# Why this file exists: `npm install` cannot complete on Linux without it.
#
# onnxruntime-node@1.22.0 ships broken install metadata for Linux. Its
# `linux/x64:cuda12` manifest asks for these paths inside the *Linux* NuGet
# package (Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime.Gpu.Linux):
#
#     runtimes/win-x64/native/libonnxruntime_providers_cuda.so
#     runtimes/win-x64/native/libonnxruntime_providers_shared.so
#     runtimes/win-x64/native/libonnxruntime_providers_tensorrt.so
#
# Those are the *Windows* paths, copy-pasted, and they do not exist in the Linux
# package — so the postinstall throws "Failed to find ... in NuGet package" and
# the whole install fails. It is not conditional on having a GPU: the manifest
# is listed for every `linux/x64` machine.
#
# npm turns this key into the `npm_config_onnxruntime_node_install` environment
# variable, which is one of the two names that package's install script reads.
# The CI workflows already set the other one (ONNXRUNTIME_NODE_INSTALL=skip) on
# every platform, so nothing that ships changes: the CUDA execution provider has
# never been downloaded in any build we have ever published. This just means a
# plain `npm install` works on a fresh clone without anyone having to know.
#
# CAVEAT, and the thing to watch: npm prints
#   npm warn Unknown project config "onnxruntime-node-install".
#   This will stop working in the next major version of npm.
# It works today — npm still exports unknown keys as npm_config_* — but npm 12 is
# expected to stop doing that. If a future npm breaks the install again with the
# NuGet error above, this is why, and the fix is to export the environment
# variable instead, exactly as the CI workflows already do:
#   ONNXRUNTIME_NODE_INSTALL=skip npm install
onnxruntime-node-install=skip
